{
  "slug": "find-first-and-last-position-of-element-in-sorted-array",
  "title": "First and Last Position of a Target",
  "topic": "binary-search",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "pattern": "binary search for the lower bound and the upper bound",
  "statement": "`nums` is an array of integers, handed over in any order. Sort its values in non-decreasing order and `target` then occupies a contiguous block of positions: those positions are the ranks of the target's occurrences. Return the smallest and the largest of them as the two-element list `[first, last]`, and return `[-1, -1]` when no value equals `target`.\n\nFor example `nums = [8, 5, 7, 8, 8, 10]` and `target = 8` gives `[2, 4]`, because the ordered values are `5, 7, 8, 8, 8, 10` and the three copies of `8` sit at positions 2, 3 and 4 once duplicates are counted from the left; the same array with `target = 6` gives `[-1, -1]`.\n\nConstraints: `1 <= nums.length <= 200`, `-10000 <= nums[i] <= 10000`, `-10000 <= target <= 10000`.\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": "nums = [-10000], target = -10000",
      "output": "[0, 0]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    },
    {
      "input": "nums = [4520, 8033, -6792], target = -8343",
      "output": "[-1, -1]",
      "explanation": "Produced by the reference solution, verified against all four implementations."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    "Function signature: `function solve(nums: number[], target: number)`.",
    "Inputs are JSON-serializable values satisfying the bounds stated above."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The two numbers you need are the rank of the first copy of the target and the rank of the last one, both measured in the array's sorted order.",
    "The array arrives unordered, so create an ordered copy first; the rank of the first copy is the number of values below the target, and the rank of the last is one less than the number of values at or below it.",
    "In an ordered array each of those ranks can be found by halving: shrink towards the left on `value < target` for the first, and on `value <= target` for the second, starting with a high end of `n` so the position past the last element is representable."
  ],
  "editorial": {
    "approach": "The target's positions in sorted order are its rank range, and the rank of one occurrence is just how many values come before it. The brute force uses that directly: for every position holding the target it counts the values smaller than the target plus the equal values that sit to its left, which takes a full scan per occurrence and O(n^2) time overall. The optimal solution orders a copy of the array once and then bisects it: a lower-bound search finds the first position whose value is not less than the target, and an upper-bound search finds the first position whose value is greater than the target, so the block of copies runs from the first result to one before the second. The lower bound also answers existence, because a value other than the target at that position means there are no copies at all.",
    "why_optimal": "Bisection is only valid on ordered data, and the array arrives in no particular order, so the optimal solution pays one O(n log n) ordering pass to create the order the rank range is defined by. After that pass, each of the two block ends costs at most log n probes instead of a scan, which is the work the brute force repeats once per occurrence. With n capped at 200 the difference is small on the judge, but the two bisections stay flat as the array grows while the pairwise count does not.",
    "pitfalls": "Halving the array as it arrives: the values come in any order, so the ordering pass has to happen before any bound can be computed, and a bisection run on unordered data returns a position that means nothing. Reading the upper bound as the last index of the block instead of one past it, which is why the search runs with `hi = n` and the answer subtracts one. Reporting an occurrence when the lower bound lands on a value that is merely larger than the target, so the equality check at that position decides between a real block and `[-1, -1]`. Treating equal copies as distinguishable ranks, when the block ends are the smallest and the largest rank the target occupies."
  },
